    "product_id": "8c4beb26-9c6a-49ab-b7dd-b0c9b9766156",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 121008\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n308 AM MST Mon Oct 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Periods of isolated showers will continue today into tonight \n  before coming to an end Tuesday morning.\n  \n- Temperatures will continue to cool through Tuesday with highs \n  dipping into the seventies, while overnight lows easily reach \n  into the fifties for Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\n- Dry weather will settle in by Wednesday before breezy to windy\n  conditions return later in the week while temperatures slowly \n  return to near normal.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nDeep troughing remains situated across much of the Western U.S.\nwith H5 heights below the 10th percentile of climatology from\nOregon through southern California. The low center is currently\nsituated across eastern Oregon with the trough axis extending\nsouthward through central California. Strong southwesterly flow\nremains in place across the Desert Southwest, gradually pulling in\ndrier air aloft. Despite the drying, there is still very adequate\nmoisture in the low levels with mixing ratios at around 10 g/kg.\nThis moist layer is expected to stick around through a good \nportion of today allowing for upslope flow to generate periods of \nshowers across the area. Although instability is quite low at \n100-200 J/kg, some of the current shower activity is showing \nmodest reflectivities and efficient rainfall rates of over 1\" per \nhour. A few spots have recorded a quick 0.25\" of rain since \nmidnight over far western portions of the Phoenix metro and into \nnorthern Yuma and southern La Paz Counties. However, these higher \nrainfall rates are not expected to last much past sunrise as drier\nair working in from the southwest will gradually push mixing \nratios closer to 8 g/kg over south-central Arizona later this \nmorning to as low as 5-6 g/kg across the western lower deserts.\n\nHi-res CAMs have been consistent in showing isolated shower \nactivity lasting through most of today with a gradual eastward \nshift in the activity. Some lower desert locations could receive \nanother few tenths of an inch today, but most will see very little\ndue to the isolated nature of the showers. The highest rainfall \namounts of 0.25-0.50\" are likely to fall across the foothills of \nnorthern Maricopa County into the high terrain northeast of \nPhoenix. The trough axis and a weak cold front are slated to move\nthrough the area this evening into tonight potentially leading to\na broken line of showers, but by that point the drier air \nadvecting in from the southwest will be an even bigger hindrance \nfor rainfall. Any lingering shower activity tonight into Tuesday \nmorning should mostly be focused over the eastern Arizona high \nterrain. \n\nAs even drier air surges through the area from west to east on \nTuesday, skies are expected to become clear to mostly clear west \nof Phoenix to partly cloudy from Phoenix eastward. We can't rule \nout some additional light showers well east of Phoenix through \nTuesday afternoon, but little additional rainfall is expected. \nTemperatures today will struggle to top 80 degrees across the \nlower deserts with even cooler air on Tuesday limiting highs to \nmostly the mid 70s.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nAs we transition into a drier weather pattern mid week, we will\nstill have to deal with periods of breezy to windy conditions. \nThe upper low is forecast to move east northeastward from northern\nArizona into Colorado on Wednesday leaving a tight pressure \ngradient across much of our area. This should bring increasing \nnorthwesterly winds across southeast California and far western \nArizona early Wednesday likely peaking late Wednesday morning \nbefore the gradient relaxes. Wind gusts of 25-30 mph can be \nexpected across much of the Lower CO River Valley Wednesday \nmorning into early afternoon. \n\nTemperatures Wednesday will remain well below normal across the\narea with a somewhat chilly start to the morning with lows in the\n50s across the lower deserts to easily into the 40s for higher \nterrain locations. Highs Wednesday should be very similar to\nTuesday, although some warming across the western lower deserts\nmay push highs back into the lower 80s. \n\nModels are finally coming into better agreement for late this\nweek into the weekend showing shortwave energy and an upper level\njet max diving southwestward through the region on Thursday. At\nthe same time a strong upper level ridge just off the West Coast\nwill move inland. For now, it seems this set up will result in a \nweak cut-off low forming somewhere to our southwest by Friday with\nridging settling in just to our north. To complicate things even \nmore, a polar air mass will quickly dive through the Plains States\nThursday potentially bringing a back-door cool front into eastern\nArizona Friday morning. Even though this is a complex pattern, it\nreally won't amount to much for our area as dry air will remain \nentrenched enough to keep rain chances below 10%. \n\nHowever, it is looking increasingly likely a strong pressure \ngradient will develop across Arizona by Friday leading to breezy \nto locally windy easterly winds developing Thursday night into \nFriday morning. NBM guidance does show the potential for Advisory\nlevel winds, but from past experience these winds tend to be \noverdone. We should at least expect occasional wind gusts of 25-35\nmph to maybe some 35-40 mph possible across far southern Gila \nCounty, Pinal County, into southern Maricopa County. These gusty \neasterly winds will be more pronounced during the latter half of \nthe morning before diminishing some during the afternoon. Guidance\nsuggests a repeat of these gusty winds on Saturday before the \ngradient begins to relax into Sunday. Moderating temperatures are\nforecast for late week with highs warming into the 80s by \nThursday and likely staying at or just below normal through next \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0525Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nGusty cross runway winds will be the primary aviation concern \nthrough the TAF period. E/SE winds will continue through the\novernight hours with a slow turn to W/SW tomorrow afternoon. By \nmid-morning tomorrow winds will develop more of a southerly \ncomponent and start gusting up around 20 kt. By the early \nafternoon (20-21Z) winds will go more southwesterly with gusts of \n20-25 kt expected through sunset. Winds will then go back SE'rly\novernight. Most of the shower activity has shut off within the \nPHX Metro this evening, but some lingering showers remain \nnear/just north of KDVT. Dry conditions are expected overnight \nthrough the majority of tomorrow. VCSH could develop as early as\n20-22Z, but confidence is low and has been removed from the TAFs.\nBetter chances for VCSH/SHRA develops late tomorrow night for the\novernight hours. Periodic showers, with a few embedded \nthunderstorms will be possible through the overnight hours. CIGs \nwill remain aoa 6-7 kft through the majority of the TAF period, \nwith FEW-SCT decks as low as 3-4 kft. Clouds should thin out a\nlittle overnight becoming more SCT, but will redevelop at BKN by\nlate tomorrow morning/tomorrow afternoon.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nGusty winds will be the main aviation concern through this TAF\npackage. Winds will predominately be out of the west at KIPL and \nout of the SW at KBLH through the TAF period. Winds will remain \nelevated (8-13 kt) overnight. Winds will then start gusting again \nby late tomorrow morning/early tomorrow afternoon (17-19Z). Wind \ngusts will be lower tomorrow but gusts around 20-25 kt are still \nexpected. FEW-SCT midlevel decks will be common through the \nperiod and may become BKN at times.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of isolated to scattered showers are expected through \ntonight before a weak cold front moves through and drier air\nbegins to filter in on Tuesday. Elevated moisture levels will \nkeep MinRHs mostly above 40% through Tuesday before gradually \nlowering into the latter part of the week. Today will bring some \nafternoon breeziness with gusts up to 25 mph at times before \ndiminishing further on Tuesday. Below normal temperatures are \nexpected through at least the middle of the week. As the drier \nconditions develop later this week, MinRH values will fall to\nbetween 25-40% by Thursday. Thursday will also bring northerly\nbreezy to windy conditions across the western districts before \neasterly breezy to locally windy conditions set up across at \nleast the eastern districts by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
